Purpose: resolve the model's requested function name into code Python can execute.

Why not send the Python functions themselves to the LLM? A Python function exists in your program's runtime. The API expects a serializable description of the tool, not the actual callable object. Your application bridges the two.

The model can request divide, but it cannot directly execute your local Python function. Your application must receive that request and decide how to handle it.


The current design has four important properties:

Separation of concerns: call_llm() handles HTTP, execute_tool() handles tool execution, ConversationMemory handles message storage, and Agent handles orchestration.

Reusable orchestration: you can create an agent and call agent.run() multiple times without rewriting the loop.

Extensible tools: you can add more functions to tool_definitions, expose their schemas through tools, and register their implementations in tool_registry.

Inspectable execution: your logs and counters let you examine model requests, tool calls, token usage, and the accumulated conversation.

There are still limitations. The current memory is not persistent across program restarts. Tool execution needs stronger function-specific argument validation before production use. Also, Agent.run() currently returns None if it reaches its iteration limit or an unexpected finish reason; API/network failures aren't yet handled comprehensively.


